WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.740
Complete spoilers for "Joker" incoming, but, y'know,

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:06.960
you clicked on a video titled "Ending Explained", so I would assume you already knew that...

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.600
Still, if I don't explicitly do this at the beginning of the video, the comments are gonna be mad about it,

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.900
so consider this your warning.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.660
So, you have a new comic book movie for me?

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.060
Yes, sir, I do.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.360
It's called "Joker".

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:20.500
Wait, wait, you're not the usual guy.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.380
Sure, I am!

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:22.540
Well, if you say so.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:23.640
So tell me about this movie.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.940
So it follows a mentally ill man named Arthur Fleck,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.980
who, y'know, is just having the worst possible luck.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:30.780
That's terrible!

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.440
He works gigs as a clown --

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:32.960
That's nice

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.960
But then gets beat up in the streets

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:35.600
That's terrible

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.240
He performs in front of sick kids at the hospital --

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:38.920
That's nice

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.100
But then drops a handgun in front of them and gets fired from the job.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.020
Wow, okay, you're really taking me on a roller coaster of emotions here /:

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.940
And that's just the beginning!

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.480
Oh, really?

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.140
Yeah, yeah, yeah!

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.980
So he gets on the subway and shoots some bankers, right?

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.940
Which starts citywide protests where all the people of Gotham want to kill the rich!

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.300
Kill the rich!

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.880
Kill the rich!

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.380
Oh, hopefully they don't learn my salary...

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.460
(laughs) Yeah, hopefully not

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.440
(Tense music)

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.860
Please stop looking at me that way

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.620
Okay, I'll stop

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.040
So, anyway, Arthur's life really starts turning around

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.760
He finds himself a new girlfriend

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.260
He gets invited onto his favorite late-night talk show

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.940
He becomes a leader of this public rebellion

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:18.740
Good for him!

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:19.700
he kills his mother,

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:20.960
his coworkers,

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.120
his favorite celebrity,

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.000
And just a lot of other people

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.420
Oh, my god, not good for him

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.900
Oh, no, no, no, don't worry,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.260
None of it actually happens

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.460
Okay, good, 'cuz for a second there --

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:31.500
Or does it?!

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:32.260
Wait, what?

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.640
You see, it all just may be vivid fantasies from Arthur's mental illness

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.000
That sounds confusing

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.220
You bet it is!

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.860
Almost like everyone is gonna have to watch an online video

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.680
explaining everything that happened in the movie to pick apart what was real from what wasn't (Meta)

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.060
I sure hope so!

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.060
Y o u W i l l W a t c h T h e W h o l e V i d e o

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.940
S-so you're gonna explain it to me?

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.460
Of course! It'll be super easy

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.580
barely an inconvenience

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:55.940
How much time d'you got?

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:01.280
Hello, Internet! Welcome to Film Theory

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.760
where "Joker" has literally just hit theaters

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.960
I was lucky enough to have Warner Brothers fly me out

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.900
to the Toronto International Film Festival

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.140
to see it a few weeks early

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.400
which is partly how I was able to get this video up so soon

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.500
But I gotta say, I haven't stopped thinking about it since that first viewing

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.640
Just like "Batman Begins" and "Iron Man" before it,

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.220
I firmly believe that this movie is going to have a huge impact.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:26.040
on the stories that superhero movies are allowed to tell for the next decade

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:26.620
It's gritty,

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:27.660
it's violent,

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:28.560
it's crass,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.060
it's heartbreaking

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.020
yet terrifying,

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.560
and, well, it's ambiguous

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:36.340
In addition to having some extreme plot twists that come at you very fast,

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:40.680
"Joker" is a film that's constantly blurring the lines between reality and fantasy

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:41.560
As a result,

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:45.740
lots of people are probably leaving the theater this weekend thrilled by what they saw,

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.500
but also, a little bit puzzled

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.420
What exactly DID they see?

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.680
What is the REAL story of Arthur Fleck?

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.380
That, my fellow moviegoers, is the question that we're exploring today

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:57.960
You see, if taken at face value,

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.760
"Joker" is a pretty self-explanatory movie

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.300
We begin with Arthur Fleck

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.480
a mentally ill man afflicted with multiple disorders

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.360
including pseudobulbar affect

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:10.060
a medical condition that causes him to spontaneously erupt into uncontrollable fits of laughter

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:11.780
he's in a desperate condition

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.600
poverty-stricken and left alone to care for his sick mother, Penny

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.840
He has big dreams of one day doing stand-up comedy like his hero,

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:19.840
the late-night talk show host Murray Franklin

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.300
But in the meantime he makes a meager living as a clown

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:26.280
doing gig work sign-spinning and performing at various events throughout Gotham City

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.280
it's thankless and brutal work

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.600
with him getting attacked in the streets by a group of young thugs

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.600
who, for whatever reason, are just really committed to sign theft

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:34.820
which, alone, would be bad enough

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.180
but then adding insult to injury he gets fined by his employer for the broken sign

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.520
Hey, it's not his fault that that sign was built in a way that it would easily explode into pieces.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.220
Seriously, it's almost like that thing was a prop or something...

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.000
and it's there that the story really begins

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.240
A coworker offers him a handgun for protection

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.420
but the gun falls out of his pants while performing for children at the hospital

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.600
getting him fired from the job altogether

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:59.260
And, can I just say, the hospital scene in this movie was brilliant

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.200
perfectly balancing tension, fear, and humor

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.800
it's just a testament to how good this movie is at balancing multiple tones

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.000
Anyway, his day just keeps getting worse

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.260
as, on the subway home, he's assaulted by a trio of rich Wall Street traders

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.540
But this time, Arthur fires back

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:15.440
Literally

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.940
Taking his handgun and reflexively killing off his first two attackers

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.000
before hunting down and finishing off the third.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.140
And it's this spark that ignites all the other plot lines of the movie

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:30.040
The first -- and biggest -- directly follows the aftermath of this trio of murders

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.880
Thomas Wayne, father of future Batman,

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.540
in the midst of his campaign to become Gotham's next mayor,

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.480
derides the crime, and in so many words,

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.700
labels the underprivileged of Gotham as "Clowns"

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:45.480
This results in the oppressed Gothamites taking the nameless killer clown as their figurehead

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.840
Adopting the clown persona for themselves

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.500
and ultimately calling for a mass take-down of the rich

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.940
All while Arthur watches the chaos that he unintentionally started

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:58.040
On the more personal front, Arthur engages in a burgeoning romance with Sophie,

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:00.520
a single mother from his apartment building down the hall

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.040
even inviting her to a stand-up routine at the Pogo's Club

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.420
The routine does not go well

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:07.920
His condition prevents him from delivering a single joke

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.460
and, as his mother says

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.260
"Well, he's just not that funny."

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:15.060
In fact, it goes so poorly that a clip of his performance goes viral

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:18.600
Or, at least, viral in, like, the 1980s New York sense

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:21.740
ending up on the very show that he and his mother loved watching:

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:24.080
Ellen -- right next to that yodeling Wal-Mart kid

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:25.800
Wait, that doesn't sound right -- let me check my notes

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:28.560
Oh, yeah -- his clip ends up on the Murray Franklin Show

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.720
where he gets ridiculed by his personal hero

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:32.540
And if that sounds bad, well, it is

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:36.320
until, of course, Murray's team reaches out to see if Arthur would like to make an appearance on the show

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:37.820
because his clip was such a huge hit

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:39.260
again, just like Ellen

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:43.160
We're gonna make fun of you in person this time, but, y'know, it's okay, because we're making you famous for 15 minutes

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:45.180
Arthur says yes to the gig

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:46.160
more on that in a minute

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:47.920
Speaking of being let down by parental figures,

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.440
his mother, Penny, used to work for Thomas Wayne

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.340
And she insists, were he to know about their desperate condition,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.440
he would be willing to offer them a helping hand

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.480
Arthur is skeptical that he would care, until,

00:05:58.500 --> 00:05:59.140
twist,

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.100
he reads a letter that his mom wrote to Thomas

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:02.780
saying that he, Arthur,

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.920
is actually Thomas Wayne's secret son

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:09.580
So he goes to Wayne Manor, which has shockingly terrible security,

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.500
creeps out Alfred and Bruce in a fantastic scene

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.820
that is honestly only there for fan service and trailers,

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.360
and then confronts Thomas Wayne at a movie premier about being his dear old dad

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.640
in the most appropriate place to have that sort of conversation,

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:21.940
the men's restroom

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.320
Talk about your awkward urinal conversations

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:25.860
But wait, double twist,

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.560
Thomas reveals that he never had a relationship with Penny

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.660
That instead, Arthur was adopted

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.540
and that it was all a last-ditch attempt to get the Wayne family to pay attention to her

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.780
Wanting to learn the truth for himself

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:39.540
because of course Thomas Wayne wouldn't own up to that sort of thing

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:42.660
he actually steals Penny's file from Arkham Asylum

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.100
only to learn that Wayne was indeed telling the truth

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.800
Not only was he adopted

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:48.920
but what's more,

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:51.400
he was abused by Penny and her boyfriend

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:52.780
chained to a radiator,

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:53.480
malnourished,

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:54.380
assaulted,

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:58.720
This neglect and trauma is largely what's to blame for his mental disorder

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:00.200
So, with this information in hand,

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.400
he promptly engages in a very aggressive pillow fight against his mother,

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:05.160
to thank her for her role in all of this

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:06.100
Penny loses. (Top 10 Fight scenes)

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:09.640
It's also important to note this major turning point in Arthur's story

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:13.140
At this point, Joker now has no identity

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:14.220
He's not a Fleck,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:15.240
He's not a Wayne,

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:18.160
He's just an adopted kid with no name on record

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.460
True to form for any good Joker

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.400
So, suffice it to say that Arthur's havin' himself a rough time

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.400
He's the key suspect in the subway killings,

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:27.900
his parentage is like a game of hot potato,

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.780
and his stand-up career is on the rocks

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.760
The only thing he has going for him

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:33.880
is that appearance on Murray Franklin

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:35.400
We see him practice his entrance

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:36.000
It's weak

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:36.760
His banter

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:37.640
It's a bit rough

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:38.800
And his one final joke

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:42.040
A knock-knock joke that Arthur doesn't fully intend to finish,

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:46.540
hoping that his death live on camera will have more meaning than his life ever did

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.940
Since he was called a "Joker" by Murray when he was mocked on the show,

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:51.540
that's the name that he adopts:

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:52.960
his clown persona

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.260
but, in a meta sense,

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.300
it's more than just a reference to that moment on the show

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.700
Remember, Arthur is now a blank slate,

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:01.360
wiped clean of the past that he thought he knew

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:05.520
The Joker persona is the only piece of identity that he has left now

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:09.360
It's the only persona that's ever made him feel noticed and empowered

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.380
Sure, it's empowerment through fear,

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:13.080
but it's empowerment nontheless

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:16.580
He physically and mentally transforms himself into the Joker,

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:19.280
and is free and unrestrained at last

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.520
This is reflected in his dance on the city steps that's on all the posters

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:26.260
Loose and wild like a tube man in front of a used-car dealership

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:29.320
Compare that to his little ballets earlier in the movie,

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:31.580
which were cautious, restrained, timid,

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:33.720
hidden in dark and abandoned bathrooms

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:34.920
He's a free man,

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.440
unleashed, unfettered

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:39.220
But for as empowering as this Joker outfit is for him,

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:41.760
To the rest of the world, it's seen as a political statement:

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:44.280
Him siding with these rioters in Gotham,

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:48.000
siding with a murderer who killed three rich people in a subway

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.700
Murray, when confronted with this decision, overrules his team's fears

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:53.600
and allows Arthur to go on despite the risk,

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.780
believing that the controversy will probably boost the popularity of the show

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.340
The show starts well enough,

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:01.200
with Joker hamming it up for the cameras in his big entrance,

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:03.340
kissing his fellow guest on the couch,

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:06.640
but quickly devolves when Murray asks Arthur to tell a Joke

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:09.820
Joker reveals that he's the subway killer clown

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:12.740
and gets to rant about how everyone is awful these days

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:15.600
how people like him have been made to be the joke of society

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.520
overlooked and mocked by everyone

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:19.560
including Murray himself

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:24.020
In other words, what he's saying is that the downtrodden are indeed the clowns here

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:26.545
And it's time for the clowns to make a change

00:09:26.545 --> 00:09:29.255
No longer interested in finishing himself off on the show,

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:32.100
he turns the gun on Murray for his final punchline

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:36.080
What do you get when you cross a mentally ill loner with a system that treats him like trash?

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:38.120
You get what you deserve

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:42.660
And with that he shoots Murray live on camera before  being dragged away by the cops

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:44.960
But spurred on by that broadcast,

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:48.760
the Gotham riots explode into a violent frenzy

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:51.740
The Wayne family flees the local opera house in fear for their lives,

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:55.180
only to be cornered in an alley by a random rioter and shot,

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:56.680
flying pearls and all

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:59.580
Joker's rescued from the police wagon by his now devout followers

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.320
and laid out, Christ-like figure on its hood,

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:04.700
When he finally comes to, he stands above them all,

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.060
painting a big smile on his face in blood

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.240
He's found his voice, he's found his audience

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:10.680
Look who's laughing now

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:12.500
The important point to note in all of this

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:13.620
is that it's all random

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:15.980
Arthur never intended to start a rebellion

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:19.200
He was just some guy who got attacked -- and attacked back -- on a subway

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.620
He didn't go onto Murray's show with a political speech ready;

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.800
in fact, he intended to do the exact opposite

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:25.920
He intended to finish himself off on camera,

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:28.980
hoping that his death would have some sort of meaning behind it

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:32.180
He had no idea what it would be, just that it would do something

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:34.180
Instead, he not only keeps himself alive,

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:37.320
he just stumbles into the words that wind up igniting the city

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:42.760
There is no big plot against the Wayne family to get retribution for the way they treated him and his mother,

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:46.080
it just so happens that a random rioter pulls the trigger,

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.060
spurred on by the Joker's words

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:51.000
It's a domino effect, completely at random

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:54.220
True to the essence of this character that is the Joker,

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:55.980
everything is chaos

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.960
For the last stinger of the movie, we cut to him in Arkham Asylum,

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:02.280
a scene that's very reminiscent of his earlier interviews with his social worker

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:06.120
We see him laughing at what he describes to be an inside joke that's, quote,

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:07.200
"Between me and him"

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:11.580
The scene then cuts to a young Bruce Wayne standing over the bodies of his dead parents in an alleyway

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:13.540
That's the "him" that this joke is for

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:15.440
As for what the joke actually is,

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:15.960
well,

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.980
As I interpret it, it's that for a while

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.120
it seemed like the two of 'em might have been related,

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:22.560
only for it to be revealed that they weren't

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:25.200
But now the two of them will forever be intertwined,

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.880
because Joker was the reason that Bruce lost his parents

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:29.380
It's pitch black irony

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:30.600
Arthur leaves the room,

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.140
leaving a trail of bloody footprints

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:34.580
before being chased around the halls by an orderly

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:36.140
So that's clear as crystal, right?

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:38.460
Why would a movie like this need any sort of explanation?

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:41.360
I mean, sure, the stuff about his parents gets a little bit confusing in the middle there,

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:44.380
And you might overlook the subtext of him losing any sense of identity,

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:46.800
only to fill that void with the Joker persona,

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:48.920
but the rest of it has a pretty logical flow, right?

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:50.440
Weeell, not quite

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:51.020
You see,

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:54.460
while a purely surface level reading of this movie absolutely works,

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:57.220
it's also not likely to be a hundred percent correct

00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:02.420
Our first indication that everything we're seeing on screen might not be exactly as it seems

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:04.440
comes fairly early on in the movie

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:05.840
Arthur comes home,

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:10.440
settles in for the night, and joins his mother for their usual evening activity of watching the Murray Franklin Show

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:15.020
except, the movie then cuts to Arthur being in the audience

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:19.480
being singled out by Murray to come up onto the stage and tell his story

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:24.640
Murray even says that if he had a son, he would want that son to be like Arthur

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.840
Clearly, this isn't a memory of anything that ever happened before

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:33.260
It's a fantasy sequence that's been seamlessly mixed in with rest of the narrative

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:36.160
And that is just the first example of this happening

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:38.180
When he's fired from his job as a clown,

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:40.460
the scene then cuts to Arthur in an alley,

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:42.460
aggressively stomping on a body,

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:44.920
before cutting back to Arthur's smiling face

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:47.560
He's fantasizing about killing his boss in that moment

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:48.920
But does he actually do it?

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:53.280
No, there are no signs that he actually does anything like that in the course of the movie

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:57.900
These scenes are setting up exactly what we predicted in our earlier "Joker" theory:

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.760
That, true to the spirit its predecessor King of Comedy,

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:05.120
This film mixes hallucination, delusion, fantasy, and reality

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:08.460
with no indication as to what is actually what

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:13.680
But perhaps the biggest, most obvious example of this blurry line between reality and fantasy

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:15.600
comes in the form of Sophie,

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:19.480
the neighbor that Arthur has a crush on and eventually pursues a romantic relationship with

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:22.580
Their early interactions are incredibly awkward

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:25.220
She tries to initiate small talk in the elevator with him,

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:27.460
and Arthur can barely manage to respond,

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:31.280
only replying minutes later in a very socially inappropriate way

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:32.420
He follows her to work,

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:33.700
and instead of being creeped out,

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:35.080
she's charmed by it

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.880
They eventually go on a series of seemingly normal dates,

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:40.700
with the awkwardness between the two of them completely gone

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:41.700
But late in the movie,

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:42.860
as the cops start closing in,

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:45.340
and as he learns the truth of his horrific childhood,

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:48.440
he enters her unlocked apartment in need of support

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:50.120
But when Sophie enters the room,

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:51.480
she's terrified

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:53.540
She doesn't identify him as Arthur,

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:55.040
or as the man that she's been dating

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:57.320
Instead, he's the guy from down the hall

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.820
It's finally then that the curtain gets pulled back

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:03.280
We have brief flashes back to the previous scenes of their relationship,

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:04.320
but this time,

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:06.480
Sophie is gone from the picture

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:09.760
The entirety of their relationship was a fabrication,

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:13.580
an elaborate series of fantasies happening inside of Arthur's head

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:18.260
It helps explain how Arthur went from awkward social loner to ladies' man seemingly overnight

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:23.160
If this was all a lie, literally anything in the movie could be

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:25.140
So, how can you tell what's what?

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.220
Well, there seem to be some definite hints,

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:29.140
with the first one being Sophie herself

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:31.840
When Arthur and Sophie visit his mother at the hospital,

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:35.380
there's a very clear, pointed moment of her leaving the room

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:39.480
right before his failed stand-up comedy routine plays on Murray Franklin Show

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:42.760
That seems to suggest that that moment is really happening

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:47.420
It's a cinematic trick indicating to us that the fantasy element -- Sophie -- has left the room,

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:50.215
leaving just Arthur and the reality of the situation:

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:52.640
his clip, playing on Murray Franklin

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:55.520
Another clue is the behavior of the people around Arthur

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:56.540
in any given scene

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:57.960
Look at their reactions,

00:14:58.000 --> 00:14:59.160
or lack thereof,

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:00.460
relative to his behavior

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:04.460
Again, this is a cinematic convention that's set up with the very first fantasy scene in the movie:

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:06.240
Him in the audience of Murray's Show

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:07.880
Before this clip from the trailers --

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:11.100
which, obviously, I can't show you, 'cause the movie is so new --

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:15.060
Arthur's behavior is inappropriate for the setting of being in the audience of a TV show

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:18.160
hence why he's been singled out from the rest of the crowd

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:20.680
And yet, for as awkwardly disruptive as he is,

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:22.760
no one around him reacts negatively

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:24.400
They're all neutral to positive

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:26.140
And then, in all these fantasy sequences,

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.500
he's embraced by all the people that he loves

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:29.220
Murray,

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:29.920
Sophie

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.320
In his fantasies, he's the hero

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:34.920
The mother in the subway car, protecting her child in the trailers?

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:36.020
Absolutely real

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:37.620
She's creeped out by him

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:39.220
His failed stand-up routine?

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:39.900
Real,

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:43.540
but what we hear the audience's reaction to be is actually fake

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:47.420
That's why, when we see this clip again, only on the Murray Franklin Show,

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:48.640
the audience is silent

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:49.800
They're not laughing

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:54.940
What he was perceiving in that moment onstage is not what was actually happening in reality

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:58.780
Him in Pogo's Comedy Club, watching another stand-up comic perform?

00:15:58.820 --> 00:15:59.460
Fake

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:04.440
We know this because he's laughing at entirely all the wrong places during the routine

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:07.220
He's laughing in the middle of jokes rather than at the end,

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:11.320
but no one in the audience around him is reacting to the disruption that he's causing

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:13.180
Those murders in the subway car?

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:14.380
Yeah, they're real

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:18.160
But now look closer at the conversations that he's having with his social worker

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:21.480
Many of the lines Arthur says get no reaction from her,

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:23.640
particularly this one about her never listening:

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:24.620
"You don't listen, do you."

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:26.680
Just like him fantasizing about killing his boss,

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:29.400
these scenes are cutting together reality and fantasy

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:31.800
Him imagining what he would say to her,

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.480
having the conversation in his mind,

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:35.720
but not always saying those words out loud

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:37.580
I mean, we've all been there,

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:38.240
right?

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:40.860
Which admittedly leads us to the biggest question of 'em all

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:41.500
The Ending

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:43.520
Does he actually go on Murray Franklin?

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:45.460
Does he actually kill his hero?

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:48.120
Does he actually become the face of a revolution?

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:51.180
The answer in my estimation is yes -- and no.

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:54.840
It honestly depends on how you want to interpret the final scene of him in Arkham Asylum

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:56.480
Based on the cinematic clues we discussed --

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:58.100
the reactions of others around him,

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:00.240
the news coverage the Murray Incident incites --

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:02.180
yeah, it does seem to be real

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:05.740
The fact that there's a scene included in the movie showing the Murray broadcast

00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:10.240
mixed in with other real world broadcasts from a neutral third party perspective

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:12.400
seems to suggest that this was a real thing

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:13.620
That moment reads to me as:

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:15.980
This horrific act of violence happening live on TV

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:18.800
is just one of thousands of moments happening on TV

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:20.580
and is just washed over and ignored,

00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:23.420
mixed in by all the noise that's happening around us all the time

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:25.140
If you're including a statement scene like that,

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:28.400
it's probably not going to be mixed in with a fantasy sequence,

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:28.920
right?

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:32.580
However, there is a case to made that most of this movie isn't real

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:34.940
The turning point really comes midway through the film

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:37.000
Arthur, during a meeting with his social worker,

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:38.980
is informed that this will be his last

00:17:38.985 --> 00:17:42.220
The scene ends with him asking where he'll be able to get his medication

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:45.220
The implication is that he won't be able to

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:47.135
And without his medication, wouldn't you know it,

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:51.580
from that point forward, we get some of the most unbelievable parts of the story:

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:53.740
His stand-up routine appearing on TV,

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:55.280
getting invited to the Murray Show,

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:59.100
easily sneaking into the movie premier to corner Thomas Wayne in the bathroom,

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:01.100
being rescued from a police cruiser,

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:04.100
surviving a crash that was fatal for everyone else in the car,

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:06.840
and then being hailed as a figurehead for this mighty rebellion

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:08.260
He becomes more articulate,

00:18:08.260 --> 00:18:09.060
more confident,

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:10.280
more driven to action,

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:11.380
more assertive

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:15.080
He outright says to other characters that he's no longer taking his meds,

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.240
and, as a result, feels better

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:21.200
All these scenes could be fabrications, set off by his untreated mental disorder

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:25.200
I mean, watch the movie again and look at the headlines of the paper

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:26.320
Kill the rich?

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:29.080
Don't get me wrong, click bait headlines are commonplace these days

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:34.180
But do you really think that papers are gonna be printing that in big, bold letters on their front page?

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:35.320
Well yeah, maybe

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:35.940
But still,

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:37.860
how about Joker's entrance on the Murray Show?

00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:42.460
This is a man who could barely walk through a curtain when he was practicing for the moment at his house,

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.940
but now he's twirling around flawlessly,

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.800
kissing other people on the show without any sort of consequence?

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:50.660
Even the way Murray talks as he interviews Joker

00:18:50.660 --> 00:18:55.580
feels reminiscent of the stilted way he addressed Arthur in the very first fantasy sequence of the movie

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:58.620
In both scenes, one of which we know is a fantasy,

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:01.120
Murray is just there asking softball questions

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:03.100
that allow Arthur to get up on his soap box

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:06.160
and say the things that he's fantasizing about saying

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:07.960
Speaking of parallel scenes,

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:10.360
consider that final moment before the credits

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:13.820
We're in Arkham Asylum, in an interview that directly parallels

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:16.720
all the scenes with the social worker from the beginning of the movie

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:18.300
We have similar camera framings,

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:19.740
a very similar actress,

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:21.360
not just in terms of race and sex,

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:24.080
but in terms of dress, hairstyle, and manner of speech,

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:27.880
Is it just cinematic shorthand to show the absurd repetition of his life,

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:33.000
OR was it to suggest that all of those earlier scenes were just him in this room?

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.960
Is the personal joke that Arthur finds so funny

00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:39.880
just him running through one elaborate fantasy during his therapy session?

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:43.320
The exact fantasy that we just watched play out

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:45.660
in extreme detail inside his own head

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:47.840
A joke where he gets consequence-free revenge

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:50.920
on literally every person who's ever wronged him

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:51.660
Bullies,

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:52.440
coworkers,

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:53.260
mothers,

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:54.260
TV hosts,

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:56.680
and ultimately, he winds up on top

00:19:56.780 --> 00:19:59.140
I don't know if there is a correct answer here

00:19:59.140 --> 00:20:00.080
Don't get me wrong,

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:03.140
just like how "Inception" has a definitive answer if you look closely enough,

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.480
"Joker" may have a definitive answer, too

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:08.480
Having only seen the movie twice, and without the ability to go frame-by-frame,

00:20:08.480 --> 00:20:10.140
it's hard for me to tell

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:11.420
and get the necessary details

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:15.360
But you can bet that I'll be seeing it multiple more times to come to a conclusion

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:16.300
Suffice it to say,

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:19.860
what I appreciate most about the film is how it manages to thread the needle

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:24.140
It gives you a backstory for this iconic character who never had one,

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:28.640
but then rips it away and makes you question literally everything that you just saw

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:30.660
True to the spirit of the Joker,

00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:34.960
this movie's origin of character is also multiple choice

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:39.280
"If I'm going to have a past, I prefer to be multiple choice."

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:40.440
But hey, (CROSSOVER CONFIRMED?!?! Nah it's actually just a meme because it felt fimiliar)

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:42.700
that's just a theory

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:45.220
Wow, that sounds super complicated

00:20:45.220 --> 00:20:46.600
No, no, no, it'll be great!

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:49.240
The Internet'll be debating about it for YEARS

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:52.540
Can we at least add like, a -- like a Batman credit card or somethin'?

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:55.540
I am not even gonna entertain that idea with a response

00:20:55.540 --> 00:20:58.120
Well, it's just that if nobody knows what's real and what's not,

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:01.260
how are we supposed to tie this together with Wonder Woman and Aquaman

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:03.160
and all those other delightful little scamps?

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:05.280
Actually, it's gonna be super easy!

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:06.820
Barely an inconvenience!

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:08.020
What -- what -- what did you say?

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:10.180
I said -- I said I have a script for you, called "Joker"

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:11.840
But -- what happened to the other guy?

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:12.940
What -- what other guy?

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:15.940
Wait -- was -- was that all just a hallucination, too?

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:19.620
*TRIPPLE TWIST*

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:22.440
Probably,  I mean, your desk is covered in strange pills

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:24.520
(laughs) Oh, yes, it certainly is

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:27.620
Then do you mind if I get to my pitch? I'm really excited about this movie

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:28.860
Oh, yeah, let's do that thing,

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:31.220
but let's do it over at our usual office, okay?

00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:33.720
You guys can get there by clicking on the box on the screen

00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:36.980
There are a bunch of videos on the Screen 
Rant YouTube Channel

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:39.920
Wha -- who are you talking to, and what's a Screen Rant Channel?

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:41.620
Did you take one of your desk pills again?

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:43.320
Oooh, so I did

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:43.880
Whoops!

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:44.380
Whoopsie

00:21:44.380 --> 00:21:44.880
But seriously,

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:48.300
Screen Rant is full hundreds of Pitch Meetings just like this one

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:52.080
I guarantee that you'll end up watching, like, at least ten in a row

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:53.420
I certainly did.

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:54.780
(laughs) We're addictive!

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:56.780
Like these pills!  :D

